date: '2026-11-22'
authors:
- Martin Visser
why_read: Read this to understand how Apache Kvrocks significantly lowers storage costs by mapping Redis and Valkey data structures onto RocksDB on disk.
hn_id: '50597648'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50597648
image: /infographics/04-hn-50597648.jpg
---

Scaling in-memory stores like Redis or Valkey into tens of terabytes quickly becomes an expensive memory management nightmare. Apache Kvrocks solves this operational bottleneck by serving the Redis Serialization Protocol directly on top of RocksDB.

Instead of paying high DRAM costs for cold keys, Kvrocks maps standard Redis data types onto an LSM-tree storage engine. The system organizes keys, sets, and hashes into distinct RocksDB column families, applying custom encodings that translate Redis collections into efficient sub-key ranges.

Because RocksDB relies on block caches and SST files, your p99 read latencies will depend heavily on cache hit ratios and disk I/O characteristics. For workloads with massive datasets and skewed access distributions, swapping pure in-memory nodes for an LSM-backed architecture can drop infrastructure costs by more than seventy percent without modifying a single client query.

Choosing disk over memory is not a regression when your access patterns favor cached working sets over brute-force RAM allocations.

