Running autonomous agent workflows that generate and execute arbitrary code exposes host systems to massive attack surfaces. Standard Linux container runtimes share a single kernel, while traditional hardware virtual machines incur prohibitive boot times and severe memory bloat at high concurrency.

gVisor approaches this execution boundary by implementing an independent application kernel written in Go directly in user space. Application system calls are intercepted and handled inside Sentry, never touching the underlying host Linux kernel directly. This design prevents kernel privilege escalation vulnerabilities from compromising the host infrastructure.

For agentic execution environments, this architectural model enables extreme multi-tenant density. Compute nodes can dynamically reclaim idle memory from sandboxes back to the host, and sub-second checkpoint and restore primitives let you pause and resume thousands of dormant agent processes without paying the memory tax of long-running virtual machines.

Securing autonomous code execution requires abandoning shared-kernel assumptions without surrendering container density.

