hn_id: '50598925'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50598925
image: /infographics/07-hn-50598925.jpg
---

Upgrading hardware to a 100 GbE link does not guarantee 100 GbE throughput. Most standard Linux kernel defaults will fall over or crash the machine well before reaching twenty gigabits per second under sustained load.

Saturating line rate requires tuning every layer between the wire and user space. Setting net.core.rmem_max and net.core.wmem_max to at least sixty-four megabytes prevents socket buffer exhaustion, while adjusting tcp_rmem and tcp_wmem accommodates large bandwidth-delay products. Standard TCP window scaling cannot keep up over high-latency pipes without these expanded thresholds.

Beyond basic sysctls, driver and hardware geometry matter far more. Maximizing RX and TX ring buffers via ethtool avoids dropped packets during burst traffic, but uncoordinated interrupt handling across NUMA nodes will ruin cache locality. Pinning network interface queues to the exact CPU cores that share PCIe lanes with the physical NIC eliminates cross-socket interconnect bottlenecks.

High-performance networking is fundamentally a systems engineering discipline where software configurations must strictly mirror hardware topology.

