---

Using a mutable handle as a primary key in decentralized protocols is one of the quickest paths to data corruption. In the AT Protocol, handles are purely cosmetic display aliases that change across servers, whereas the Decentralized Identifier (DID) provides the actual immutable identity.

Protocol subtleties run deep. Because DIDs follow URI specifications, schemes are case-insensitive. A naive string equality check will fail unless incoming DIDs are strictly normalized to lowercase before database persistence and protocol verification. Assuming that handle transitions preserve root hostnames is equally dangerous when users migrate between independent Personal Data Servers.

Designing distributed protocols demands strict discipline around identity guarantees, canonical data serialization, and immutable keys.

