Most LLM reasoning pipelines rely on chain-of-thought token generation to tackle hard problems. That approach works, but generating dozens of reasoning tokens per decision introduces massive latency and token bloat into production agent loops.

Inkling-2-Mini Decision tests an alternative architecture. Built as a 26.4B MoE with 3.4B to 5.4B active parameters across 24 physical layers and a 12-layer looped core, it answers typed decision queries without generating prose or JSON tokens. Instead, the model evaluates state in a single pass and dynamically decides whether to spend 1, 2, or 3 internal loops (24, 36, or 48 logical layers) before outputting a result.

Under early-exit routing at confidence threshold 0.90, 54.2 percent of routine questions exit after loop 1, taking 81.4 milliseconds median latency. For hard out-of-distribution questions, 76.8 percent automatically loop through all 3 stages, jumping accuracy from 42.6 percent to 66.8 percent on tough evaluations. That matches dense 27B-class decision accuracy while saving up to 5x active prefill compute.

Dynamically scaling latent compute inside the model beats generating throwaway tokens for routine agent decisions.

