---

Conventional database wisdom assumes heavier write loads increase replication delay on read replicas. In PostgreSQL, the exact opposite often occurs: low-traffic primaries experience far higher perceived replica lag than busy ones.

The culprit lies in WAL flush mechanics. When write traffic is low, PostgreSQL does not fill 16 megabyte WAL segments quickly. If your streaming configuration falls back to segment archiving, replica updates stall waiting for WAL chunks to flush, creating artificial latency spikes on quiet systems.

Setting archive_timeout to 5 seconds and dialing wal_retrieve_retry_interval down to 500 milliseconds forces frequent flushes of partial WAL files. This keeps standby nodes tightly synchronized without needing synthetic write injection, though you must account for the increased network and storage I/O.

Counterintuitive replication latency is almost always a batching artifact rather than an engine bottleneck.

