hn_id: '50595658'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50595658
---

Engineers often monitor iowait as an indicator of disk bottlenecks, but inside the Linux kernel, iowait is not a distinct process state. It is simply idle CPU time accounted to a CPU while at least one runnable thread is waiting on fast storage I/O.

The kernel tracks this using an in_iowait flag on task_struct. When a task waking from I/O has this bit set, the scheduler aggressively boosts the CPU frequency governor, operating on the heuristic that short I/O operations require rapid CPU attention to prevent downstream throughput stalls.

This heuristic frequently breaks on modern flash arrays and high-throughput network file systems. When fast NVMe devices complete requests in microseconds, waking tasks can artificially peg CPU governors at peak frequencies, wasting energy and triggering unnecessary cache churn without improving latency. Proposed kernel patches aim to decouple governor scaling from raw wait flags in favor of tracked wake latency and actual runtime demand.

Understanding how your scheduler interprets I/O wait is critical when sizing high-throughput database nodes and diagnosing unexpected CPU utilization spikes.
